========================================================================
C10 — sec_01 fixture honesty: forged NetCap trap vs https residual string
========================================================================
VERDICT: FAIL (dishonest / does not prove forged NetCap trap)

Evidence fixture:
  /home/jeryd/Projects/openOODA/qa/tests_security/sec_01_forged_capability_trap.oo
  L1-2 header claims: "without a NetCap token MUST trap"
  L4-6: fetch("https://evil.example")  — single arg, no NetCap
  L10-12 asserts:
    r.is_err() == true
    e.contains("https residual: use http:// or external TLS")

Evidence product surfaces:
  ooda/std/os/net.oo L5: "sealed builtin fetch accepts NetCap then url"
  ooda/oodac/c_emit_lower.oo L155-158: fetch requires &NetCap first arg;
    else ERR + process_exit(1) at emit time
  ooda/runtime/chs_rt_sys.c L155: oo_cap_require_net(cap,"fetch") → exit(1)
    on missing/forged token (never returns Err to caller)
  ooda/runtime/chs_rt_sys.c L160-162: https residual string is returned ONLY
    AFTER cap check succeeds, for https:// URLs

Honesty analysis:
  1. Assertion string is the post-cap https residual path — NOT a forged-cap message.
  2. True forged/missing NetCap either fails emit (no NetCap arg) or process-exits
     in oo_cap_require_net before any Result assertion can run.
  3. Fixture therefore cannot prove a forged-NetCap trap at runtime; it only
     documents/asserts an https residual error string (and may not even be
     compilable as written without a NetCap first arg).

Conclusion: claim that sec_01 "proves forged NetCap trap" is FALSIFIED.
It only targets the https residual string (or is structurally unreachable).

